I went looking through old websites expecting a little technological nostalgia. Instead, I found the same question I am working on now: how should information be arranged so someone can find it, understand it and connect it to something else?

The web record I can recover runs from 2002 to 2026. The tools changed from static HTML and Flash to content models, semantic embeddings and server-rendered SVG. That focus has stayed consistent.

## Brace yourself for some meditative navel gazing

My first website was a GeoCities page I made in high school around 1997. I may never find it again. My memory is that I first edited HTML in HotDog, a Windows editor [released in 1995](https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/software/hotdog-1-0-in-1995).

What I remember is not the page but the reaction. A friend and I found it genuinely mindbending that we could make a thing and have it be *there*, addressable by anyone, without asking permission or paying a gatekeeper.

The first substantial site I can recover is Environmental Media. The Wayback Machine kept a [2002 snapshot of the site](https://web.archive.org/web/20020120145620/https://envmedia.com/), which I built in 2001 with search, Flash video and an online shop. The implementation is very much of its time; the interest in helping people navigate different kinds of information is less so.

An [October 2002 snapshot of Stuffed in a Box](https://web.archive.org/web/20021012212136/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/) preserves a mostly "Coming Soon" site for my tiny web-design service. By April 2003, an [online résumé](https://web.archive.org/web/20030425093346/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/portfolio/resume/index.htm) listed Stuffed in a Box Design as a self-owned business and used proper em dashes. For the record, the dash habit predates generative AI by more than twenty years.

The blog arrived on [5 September 2003](https://web.archive.org/web/20050206010037/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/archives/000004.html). Its first post, "I've done and will do," promised observations about newspapers, infographics, technology, politics and history. A post the following September admitted that it had been nearly a year since the last one, but the intended subjects are recognizable.

Flickr supplies more breadcrumbs, two of them a month apart. In November 2006 I posted [a newspaper page I had laid out](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/301936689/): every school in five South Carolina districts, rated across four years of state report cards.

That graphic is at the top of this post. The state published words rather than numbers: excellent, good, average, below average, unsatisfactory, for every school, four years running. Words do not stack up. You cannot skim two hundred of them and come away with a shape. Stars you can count, and counting is what a parent looking for their own school actually does.

A month later I uploaded [a close-up of a pinched letterform](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/337710045/): I was already stopping to admire ink traps in 2006. Something I found myself dwelling on again twelve years later, [when considering fonts for digital data](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html).

## Reading the archive instead of scrolling it

The archive on this site starts much later, in 2013. It now contains roughly a hundred entries written on and off across different jobs, tools and versions of the site. That is enough to compare topics and find passages that resemble one another. It is also enough for a conventional reverse-chronological blog to hide most of what is there.

The redesign began with typography, graph paper and a more technical datasheet vocabulary. Posts gained document references. The [archive gained a timeline](/archive/), while [topic pages](/topics/) and small activity charts made it possible to browse by subject rather than only by publication date.

